# A pool holds up to a fixed number of values that fibers share: a fiber
# takes one out, uses it, and gives it back. Taking waits while the pool is
# empty and giving waits while it is full, each parked by the scheduler until
# another fiber gives or takes. A take answers some value in the pool; no
# order is promised, though a given program takes them in the same order on
# every run (#2264). Nothing here is about connections: a connection pool is
# a pool filled with connections.
pub type pool
  handle

# An empty pool that holds up to `n` values. It is an effect because what it
# answers is state the fibers that hold it share.
pub fn make n
  builtin_pool_make n .> made

fn made h
  pool h

pub fn take p
  builtin_pool_take p.handle

pub fn give p v
  builtin_pool_give p.handle v

# Takes a value, hands it to `work`, and gives it back whether the work
# succeeds or fails, so a failure part way through cannot lose it. A failure
# still rises, with the reason and birthplace it had.
pub fn with p work
  take p .> (v -> lent p v work)

fn lent p v work
  work v
    .? (u -> give p v .> (_ -> effect u))
    .> (r -> give p v .> (_ -> effect r))
